Preclusion and Representation
So far, our cases on preclusion have traced the expanding scope of claim and issue preclusion. With respect to issue preclusion, the mutuality requirement gave way to a doctrine that permits, in many cases, someone who did not participate in an earlier case to invoke preclusion against a party who did. But taboo in all of those cases was the reverse: a party to an earlier case invoking preclusion against someone who did not participate in the earlier case. It is here that we reach the outer limits of preclusion: Can someone who did not participate in a case as a party be bound by its judgment?

And it is here, at the frontier of preclusion doctrine, that we encounter the concept of representation, such that this frontier represents the outer limits of joinder, too. Joinder clearly defines who was, and was not, a party in a case, thereby providing a clear scope for the application of preclusion doctrines. But can someone be bound by an earlier judgment, even if they were not joined as a party in that action? And therefore: Can someone be precluded from litigating an issue, or even an entire claim, because of a judgment in an earlier case in which they were not named a party?

These questions lead us inexorably to the study of the class action device, whereby one plaintiff or a small number of plaintiffs named as parties litigate the case as representatives of a class of persons who do not participate in the action—and may not even have notice of the proceedings.

But before we turn to class actions, we must find the boundaries beyond which the doctrines of claim preclusion and issue preclusion shall not pass. When does a judgment, not in a class action, bind individuals who were not joined as parties in that action? And when does a judgment in a class action not bind individuals within the scope of the class defined in that action? Taylor v. Sturgell addresses the first of these questions. Hansberry v. Lee addresses the second.
